WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.420
Blood carries far signals, tiny gaps pass fast ones, and receptors choose who

2
00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:09.960
answers. Once a message turns a cell on, what shuts it off?

3
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.860
A signal that cannot end is a switch stuck on.

4
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.790
One way cells end signals is by clearing the messenger.

5
00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:23.730
Enzymes can destroy it outside.

6
00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:28.830
Transporters can pump it back into the sender or nearby glia.

7
00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:33.990
Blood flow or diffusion out of a tiny gap also sweeps it away.

8
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:40.010
Each route sets lifetime, called τ: the average time one molecule survives.

9
00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:45.730
A signal that loses the same fraction each second has no sharp edge.

10
00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:53.310
How fast a molecule wanders is its diffusion coefficient, D. Reach, where the signal

11
00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:57.270
falls to about a third, is near the square root of √(D × τ).

12
00:00:58.283 --> 00:01:00.003
Here is the guiding tradeoff.

13
00:01:01.343 --> 00:01:06.123
Clearance sets a messenger's lifetime, and lifetime buys reach at the cost of tempo.

14
00:01:07.563 --> 00:01:11.183
When release stops, concentration falls on that lifetime scale.

15
00:01:12.663 --> 00:01:15.603
Longer life reaches farther, yet takes longer to clear.

16
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:18.307
Test that bargain in water.

17
00:01:19.587 --> 00:01:25.427
A secreted protein lasting 4 seconds fades over about 20 µm, one or two cells.

18
00:01:26.707 --> 00:01:30.107
Now slow clearance 25 times, to 100 seconds.

19
00:01:31.287 --> 00:01:33.867
Does it reach 25 times farther, or much less?

20
00:01:39.883 --> 00:01:46.663
Much less. Reach grows as the square root of lifetime, and the square root of √25 =

21
00:01:46.663 --> 00:01:54.863
5. So the reach grows only 5 times, from 20 to about 100 µm, or five to ten cells.

22
00:01:56.523 --> 00:01:58.623
Meanwhile decay slows 25 times.

23
00:01:59.633 --> 00:02:01.553
Real tissue is not water.

24
00:02:03.233 --> 00:02:07.153
Matrix fibers and receptors bind a drifting protein, slowing its spread.

25
00:02:08.433 --> 00:02:14.013
At equal lifetime, a thousandfold lower diffusion coefficient shrinks reach about 30

26
00:02:14.013 --> 00:02:18.113
-fold. The square root of a thousand is about 32.

27
00:02:19.993 --> 00:02:23.193
Binding can also protect the molecule and lengthen its life.

28
00:02:24.013 --> 00:02:26.933
Either way, a signal to neighbors stays local.

29
00:02:28.067 --> 00:02:30.567
The synapse takes this tradeoff to an extreme.

30
00:02:31.887 --> 00:02:37.907
There an enzyme, acetylcholinesterase, destroys the transmitter acetylcholine within

31
00:02:37.907 --> 00:02:39.067
about 1 millisecond.

32
00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:44.307
The same square-root rule gives only a rough scale, near 1 µm.

33
00:02:45.747 --> 00:02:48.687
Wiping the gap clean keeps rapid impulses distinct.

34
00:02:50.087 --> 00:02:53.087
Other synapses clear serotonin by pumping it back.

35
00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.680
Circulation, not diffusion, sets a hormone's range.

36
00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:02.560
Still, clearance helps set how fast it fades once secretion stops.

37
00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.780
Epinephrine falls by half in roughly 2 to 3 minutes, fading unless the gland keeps

38
00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:09.780
sending.

39
00:03:10.283 --> 00:03:12.903
Ending a message shapes the message itself.

40
00:03:14.383 --> 00:03:19.283
Fast removal keeps local signals brief and contained, while lingering buys range

41
00:03:19.283 --> 00:03:20.903
only by slowing recovery.

42
00:03:22.263 --> 00:03:25.323
What happens when a drug blocks the enzyme or transporter?
